{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And the woman saw that it was good, etc.</b> It is difficult: how can it be justified to perceive the qualities of a food through seeing? Further, it is difficult: why did he place the perception of the mouth before the perception of the eye? Surely the eye would perceive before the faculty of taste could discern, and if so, before she ate, the knowledge of desire to the eyes had already preceded — and from the appearance of things it would be fitting to say first 'for it is a desire to the eyes'? Further, from where did she know that there is intelligence in it? And if he is saying this to justify the words of the serpent, it should not have said 'and the woman saw' concerning something she did not see. Further, why did he elaborate to say two superfluous words — the word <b>hu</b> and a second instance of the word <b>ha-etz</b> in the verse? Further, why did he say the word 'the tree' in the third clause and not in the second clause?" "<b>Indeed,</b> the verse tells the manner of the woman's snare and how she consented to listen to the seducer. Now, you will find that in the order of her first words to the serpent she was precise to say ״וּמִפְּרִי הָעֵץ אֲשֶׁר בְּתוֹךְ הַגָּן״ — this tells her understanding and knowledge, that only the fruit itself was forbidden to her, but the wood of the tree, its branches and its leaves were not forbidden. And this reasoning we can say arose in her in one of two ways: either that Adam, in commanding her, commanded her in this formulation; or that even though he said simply ״עֵץ הַדַּעַת״ etc., she reasoned in her mind that the El would not command except concerning the fruit, for the wood has no substance and a commandment would not apply to it. And we have already cited the words of our predecessors of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 15) that all the trees of the garden had no taste in the wood except for the Tree of Knowledge, whose taste of its wood and the taste of its fruit were equal. And here the verse informs us that through the seducer's words to her, Chavah examined, and she sent her hand and ate from the wood — not from the fruit, because she thought there was no prohibition in the matter, for one of the two reasons we have written, and she was not concerned about touching, for touching was forbidden only in the fruit itself and not in the tree. Or it is possible that she dismissed the prohibition of touching in her mind according to the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 19), who said that he pushed her and she touched and did not die — so she dismissed the prohibition of touching, since it did not come explicitly in the words of HaShem." "<b>And the woman saw that the tree was good.</b> Meaning: in the body of the tree and its branches she saw a quality different from all the trees of the field — this was the first observation. And since she tasted a difference in the wood, she set her mind to look at the appearance of the fruit, which she had not examined before. When she decided in her mind to eat it, when she saw the difference, she set her mind and saw that the fruit was a desire to the eyes — and this is what the verse precisely states by saying <b>and that it was a desire to the eyes.</b> It is precise in saying the word 'hu' — it refers back to the fruit, for if it referred back to the tree there would have been no need to say 'hu' but rather 'and that it is a desire to the eyes'; and it is understood that it refers back to the tree, as we have analyzed above. And when he informed us of the perception of intelligence, he said 'and the tree was desirable to make one wise' — it is precise in saying 'the tree,' for there was no adjacent phrase available, since the adjacent phrase speaks of the fruit, and only the perception recognizable without tasting was said of it. And this category of intelligence is not perceived through the sense of sight but through the intermediary of eating; therefore the verse is precise in saying <b>and the tree was desirable</b> — meaning she ate in order to become wise, for she perceived in it the intelligence. And through this the words of the Satan were verified in her eyes, for he had told her the reason HaShem separated them from eating of it was so they would not be equal to Him. <b>And she took of its fruit and ate</b> — it is precise in saying 'of its fruit,' for from its wood she had already eaten before. And through this all the verses and all the fine points we analyzed are properly resolved. Also, the commentary sometimes mentions 'tree' and sometimes 'fruit.' And what she gave to her husband was out of affection, so that he too would become like Elohim, etc." "<b>And it appears to me</b> to give a good reason, that Chavah is not so much to blame, for she did not think there was any creature in the world that HaShem had created to distinguish through it the love of His creations — namely the serpent, in which the Satan clothed himself. And had she known this, she would have been wary of him and would not have given ear to hear his words, as righteous people do today who do not incline an ear to his enticements, even though he multiplies the eloquence of his tongue and the exaggeration of desire for the delicacies of forbidden things. And this is because they recognize that he is evil and deceit — he descends and seduces, he ascends and prosecutes. And she, the poor woman, did not enter her mind that there exists such a creature in the world to test her. And in addition, she perceived in tasting the wood, since it was permitted according to her reasoning, and she saw that it was extraordinary for the better, and combined with her understanding that was lighter than Adam's. But had she known with our knowledge that HaShem created the Satan to test His beloved ones, or if she had tasted the wood while it was forbidden and had not tasted it, she would not have sent her hands to eat." "<b>Further,</b> it appears to give a reason for Chavah's haste to transgress the word of HaShem, namely that she thought the acceptance of this commandment was in error, for she did not think that this tree was so important when the commandment was imposed upon her. And you will find that at the giving of the Torah, how many covenants HaShem made with Israel over the Torah — this tells that it is a matter of necessity, and the commandment would not suffice if one could retract from it. And you will find further that they said of blessed memory (Shabbat 88a) that in the days of Mordechai they fulfilled what they had already accepted, because the first oath had been under duress, for He held the mountain over them like a barrel — this tells that an oath was necessary. From this point on, Chavah, who did not swear, has no obligation. And even though she accepted it, and her declaration to the Most High constitutes a vow — with all this, it was an acceptance in error. And behold, before you is what they said of blessed memory in tractate Nedarim chapter 9 (Nedarim 66b), and this is their language: 'Konam wine that I do not taste, that wine is bad for the intestines — they said to him: but aged wine is good for the intestines — it is permitted' etc. 'in all wines' etc., end quote. And this is precisely the situation before us: even though she had accepted the matter upon herself, since she saw what had not been in her mind at the time of acceptance, as it says 'and the woman saw that the tree was good' etc." "<b>And now</b> it has been revealed to us that two causes brought about the woman's stumbling: the first is her assumption that only the fruit had been forbidden to them and not the tree — on this account she sent her hand and tasted the tree, and the serpent's words were fulfilled for her. And the second is that at the time of the commandment she did not know that the tree makes those who eat of it wise, for had she known at the outset of the commandment that on this condition the Creator commands her, there would have remained no opening for the inciter once she had accepted upon herself not to eat after she knew its virtue." "<b>Now behold,</b> the Master YHWH of Hosts knew what lay in darkness — namely the serpent, who would come with these arguments; and with chosen silver the tongue of the righteous stripped away these two things by His saying (Genesis 2:17): ״וּמֵעֵץ הַדַּעַת טוֹב וָרָע לֹא תֹאכַל מִמֶּנּוּ״ — see how He was precise to say ״וּמֵעֵץ״ to forbid even the tree itself, and He also informed them that it makes one wise and contains the knowledge to discern good and so on, and on this condition He decreed that they not eat even from the tree. And with this there is no room for the inciter to incite, except that the man did not say the matters as written to the woman, as is evident from within her words — as we examined precisely from what she said to the serpent, that the prohibition is on the fruit, and she was not precise to say ״tree of knowledge״; likewise from the verse's saying ״וַתֵּרֶא הָאִשָּׁה״ and so on, ״וְנֶחְמָד הָעֵץ לְהַשְׂכִּיל״ — this declares that she had no prior knowledge." "And the reason the man did not say to Chavah [these things] is that he did not wish to inform her that it possesses this virtue of making those who eat of it wise, so as not to magnify its desirability in her heart, and he did not know that the enemy was in the chamber. Moreover, he did not trouble himself to tell her of the prohibition of the tree, for he thought there was no point in commanding her concerning it. And even though HaShem had said to him ״וּמֵעֵץ הַדַּעַת״, he thought this was not meant precisely, and the intent was concerning what is on the tree, namely the fruit, for regarding the tree he had already grasped from other trees that it has no taste and the command would not apply to it. And this error was born from the error of the earth, which had changed the tree of knowledge from the other trees of the garden; and see what we explained in its place (Genesis 1:12). And therefore Chavah, the man, and the earth were all stricken, for all of them erred, and Chavah's error was the greatest of all."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:39:24.320673569Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}